#, fuzzy, python-format
#~| msgid ""
#~| "<p>We use a number of terms to describe software available on PyPI, like "
#~| "\"project\", \"release\", \"file\", and \"package\". Sometimes those "
#~| "terms are confusing because they're used to describe different things in "
#~| "other contexts. Here's how we use them on PyPI:</p> <p>A \"project\" on "
#~| "PyPI is the name of a collection of releases and files, and information "
#~| "about them. Projects on PyPI are made and shared by other members of the "
#~| "Python community so that you can use them.</p> <p>A \"release\" on PyPI "
#~| "is a specific version of a project. For example, the <a "
#~| "href=\"%(requests_href)s\">requests</a> project has many releases, like "
#~| "\"requests 2.10\" and \"requests 1.2.1\". A release consists of one or "
#~| "more \"files\".</p> <p>A \"file\", also known as a \"package\", on PyPI "
#~| "is something that you can download and install. Because of different "
#~| "hardware, operating systems, and file formats, a release may have "
#~| "several files (packages), like an archive containing source code or a "
#~| "binary <a href=\"%(wheel_href)s\">wheel</a>.</p>"
#~ msgid ""
#~ "<p> We use a number of terms to describe software available on PyPI, like "
#~ "\"project\", \"release\", \"file\", and \"package\". Sometimes those "
#~ "terms are confusing because they're used to describe different things in "
#~ "other contexts. Here's how we use them on PyPI: </p> <p> A \"project\" on "
#~ "PyPI is the name of a collection of releases and files, and information "
#~ "about them. Projects on PyPI are made and shared by other members of the "
#~ "Python community so that you can use them. </p> <p> A \"release\" on PyPI "
#~ "is a specific version of a project. For example, the <a "
#~ "href=\"%(requests_href)s\">requests</a> project has many releases, like "
#~ "\"requests 2.10\" and \"requests 1.2.1\". A release consists of one or "
#~ "more \"files\". </p> <p> A \"file\", also known as a \"package\", on PyPI "
#~ "is something that you can download and install. Because of different "
#~ "hardware, operating systems, and file formats, a release may have several "
#~ "files (packages), like an archive containing source code or a binary <a "
#~ "href=\"%(wheel_href)s\">wheel</a>. </p>"
#~ msgstr ""
#~ "<p> Na popis softvéru dostupného v PyPI používame niekoľko výrazov, "
#~ "napríklad \"projekt\", \"uvoľnenie\", \"súbor\" a \"balík\". Niekedy sú "
#~ "tieto pojmy mätúce, pretože sa používajú na opis rôznych vecí v iných "
#~ "kontextoch. Tu je návod, ako ich používame na PyPI:</p> <p>\"Projekt\" na "
#~ "PyPI je názov kolekcie vydaní a súborov a informácie o nich. Projekty na "
#~ "PyPI vytvárajú a zdieľajú ostatní členovia komunity Pythonu, aby ste ich "
#~ "mohli používať. </p> <p> \"Vydanie\" na PyPI je špecifická verzia "
#~ "projektu. Napríklad projekt <a href=\"%(requests_href)s\">requests</a> má "
#~ "mnoho vydaní, napríklad \"požiadavky 2.10\" a \"požiadavky 1.2.1\". "
#~ "Vydanie pozostáva z jedného alebo viacerých \"súborov\". </p> <p> "
#~ "\"Súbor\", tiež známy ako \"balík\", na PyPI je niečo, čo si môžete "
#~ "stiahnuť a nainštalovať. Vzhľadom na odlišný hardvér, operačné systémy a "
#~ "formáty súborov môže vydanie obsahovať niekoľko súborov (balíkov), "
#~ "napríklad archív obsahujúci zdrojový kód alebo binárny <a "
#~ "href=\"%(wheel_href)s\">wheel</a>. </p>"

